#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550059} :activity/id #uuid "4a76ab2b-fb4c-5063-aa81-3107b8d38d69" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048270 :topic/id #uuid "8e43115f-4416-5ec0-b698-3e53a959567b" :topic/math-academy-id 3286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e9c58b-4fb9-5497-9482-e8f285727015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549479} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c5c15-f6a2-5b4a-a895-22168d05307d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549499} {:answer/id #uuid "8a26048a-fd81-56ee-8af6-bd08ebb325b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.047" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549578} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ac414e-15b9-5ce9-93e3-a7f2a6d829da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.019" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549625} {:answer/id #uuid "c97eb869-625e-551f-be3b-c0ff2ec27876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549690} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9e6faf-9e8f-5d9c-aa84-79845b127ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1787d264-9d95-57d2-8330-7eec4c052cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-148271" :question/problem "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $2.8%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $48%$ of children in the entire sample are female, $2.6%$ of female children have scoliosis, and $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practices $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C∣{S}_{0})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C∣{S}_{2})=0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $48%$ of students in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.48.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.6%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C∣F)=0.026.\n$$\n\n- Since $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F∣C)=0.46.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis, which is represented as $P(C∣{S}_{1}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C∣{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C∣{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C∣{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{(0.026)(0.48)}{(0.25)(0.025)+P(C∣{S}_{1})(0.3)+(0.028)(0.45)} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C∣{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.46(0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})) & =0.0125 \\\\ 0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46} \\\\ 0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189 \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.3}(\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189) \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & ≈0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "39ce452f-966f-5775-99e0-20fb8ca0eef9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161fb25c-96cf-5b02-8d7d-25af5ca8f28a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.365" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549357} {:answer/id #uuid "3abf36e3-3cf2-5339-8e78-9d57d7acb48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.286" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549435} {:answer/id #uuid "408cef73-e79b-5f84-909b-bb78b0af2709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.322" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549446} {:answer/id #uuid "c7987182-3703-5340-83e1-52554f33e28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.444" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549688} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1644a5-6db3-535c-a833-2c384185599d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549693} :answer-field/id #uuid "266b7c98-bb0c-5250-98ae-54d840c489c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9c281bbc-e5bb-5c0b-9b81-0eabf770ee2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-147885" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.25, & P({A}_{1})=0.28 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.3, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.28, & P({A}_{3})=0.18 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=0.1, & P({A}_{4})=0.22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}) & =1-(P({A}_{1})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(0.28+0.18+0.22) \\\\  & =0.32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.3)(0.32)}{(0.25)(0.28)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.28)(0.18)+(0.1)(0.22)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.096}{0.2384} \\\\  & ≈0.403.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5af77563-9292-5cfd-aaa2-e0647be6b4b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144c9c56-d288-5656-9e9a-114c97ea9460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.348" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549350} {:answer/id #uuid "39018054-1073-5cec-87c0-3be506864193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549432} {:answer/id #uuid "7aae2ee8-a81d-50e2-98ea-e2df978f83a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549546} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd4d43d-b30b-5f97-8032-3941bf17d1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549552} {:answer/id #uuid "85700ac7-cad1-590c-a143-b418ccc70b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549350} :answer-field/id #uuid "af85ec6e-7016-53c3-802c-9198ac759320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "095773d5-faca-5dcc-94ed-85e3db151ccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-148082" :question/problem "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $3.3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, and $2%$ of female children have scoliosis, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practice $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C|{S}_{0})=0.03 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 &  & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.52.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.02.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.02)(0.52)}{(0.03)(0.25)+(0.025)(0.3)+(0.033)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0104}{0.0299} \\\\  & ≈0.348,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "964218e8-bf1c-559e-ad24-e50227ec5968" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c32fc9e-ce9a-535b-a8b3-06c486c6cd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486898} {:answer/id #uuid "a059a8b3-f1ed-5de8-a334-40e4ba40f83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.602" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486959} {:answer/id #uuid "a6776cbd-ebfc-53b8-ad96-0550911cd2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.688" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486972} {:answer/id #uuid "ec76cd0f-4509-5d07-9c06-06e62501a6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.672" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487097} {:answer/id #uuid "efaec769-99d0-583b-aad4-773115f51b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.668" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486898} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e23228-7c0d-5766-b1ab-6e8ce201deb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a806821e-50df-52c7-a557-34976fdac21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-148079" :question/problem "An English teacher believes that students who prefer cursive handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove his belief, he puts some students into groups of three according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in cursive.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades, and $12%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $15%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $17%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\nIf $56%$ of students are female, and $16%$ of female students write in cursive, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that the student writes in cursive, and $L,A,H$ be the events that the student has low, average, or high grades, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 &  & P(W|L)=0.12 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.15 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.56.\n$$\n\n- Since $16%$ of female students write in cursive, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.16.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female, which is represented as $P(F|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.16)(0.56)}{(0.2)(0.12)+(0.65)(0.15)+(0.15)(0.17)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0896}{0.147} \\\\  & ≈0.610,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "da95ad9e-2b99-5840-9da1-16c4078a085a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ee0324-47ce-520c-be1b-617fc28bde99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486628} {:answer/id #uuid "1f964113-d9c4-53ce-a730-891983a25308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486654} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd21a5c-17af-557a-936c-d9cb120f8e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486762} {:answer/id #uuid "56ac6ed1-d21c-50b6-90e0-44a21cfc1766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486783} {:answer/id #uuid "8146efe8-c4c6-5ac7-a7c5-ddfd418eda3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486762} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75bc298-c38f-59f1-b1e6-e063f2062f22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f697c855-b9dd-5b7e-bb24-b23154c897b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-148085" :question/problem "A gambling room has three roulette tables, table $1,$ table $2,$ and table $3.$ Data of all bets of a particular evening are collected.\n\n- $30%$ of bets were played at table $1,$ and $5%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $2,$ and $6%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $3,$ and $7%$ of those bets were won.\n\nIf $10%$ of bets were played in the first hour, and $4%$ of bets made in the first hour were won, what is the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet was played in the first hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a bet is played in the first hour, $W$ be the event that a bet is won, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a bet is played at table $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{T}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{3})=0.07\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.35+0.35=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of bets in the entire evening are played in the first hour, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of bets made in the first hour are won, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.04.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet is played in the first hour, which is represented as $P(F|W).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(W|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(W|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.1)}{(0.05)(0.3)+(0.06)(0.35)+(0.07)(0.35)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0605} \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "fce198a3-2fe3-5172-8c78-1731bf8adc70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550043}}] :activity/title "Extending Bayes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549303}]}
